        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="_RGwDC88Wdcd8SNDBt_c-95" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;fillColor=#F1FEFF;" value="&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255)); background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;There are 2 tables which are completely identical namely: &#39;erp_bridgeencounteradditionalservice&#39; and &#39;erp_patient_additionalservice&#39;. Because of this, I decided to put them as on table &#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_bridgeencounteradditionalservice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&#39; in the silver layer..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Also, I was unable to find a way to join the table &#39;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;erp_diminsurancepackages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&#39; to the table map above so I just included it in the visual without any direct link to any other table. It just provides more info about the conditions that are partially covered accross all the 3 insurance packages.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;I found another 2 tables whose contents are of the same kind. (&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_factspecialtests &amp;amp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_dimspecialtest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;). They share the same table structure (both column names and type of data) but contain different data in the rows meaning that one is an extension of the other. That said, I decided to join the 2 tables via a union all operation into the silver layer as &#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_factspecialtests&#39;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;There are 3 tables which were initially one table but somehow broken into 3 tables. The tables are &#39;erp_dimtreatment&#39;, &#39;erp_facttreatmentcost&#39; and &#39; erp_facttreatment&#39;. After some investigation, i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;t is evident that &#39;erp_dimtreatment&#39; and &#39;erp_facttreatmentcost&#39; were one table but somehow it was broken into these 2 tables&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;along the columns. And furthermore, the initial table before it was broken, was a part of the table &#39;erp_facttreatment&#39;. (broken along the rows). So I have joined the tables &#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_dimtreatment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&#39; and &#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_facttreatmentcost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&#39; into one table via a left join using the columns Encounter_ID and Treatment_ID. I then joined the result from the join onto the table &#39;erp_facttreatmentcost&#39; using the union all function. The tables have become one table in the silver layer (&#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;erp_facttreatment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&#39;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;I chose to omit the table &#39;mssilver.erp_bridgeencounter doctor&#39; from the Gold layer given that the bridge was completely unnecessary. The central fact table &#39;msgold.fact_encounter&#39; already has a column &#39;responsible_doctor_id&#39; which can be used to join the table to the &#39;dim_doctor&#39; dimension.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;I also chose to omit the &#39;mssilver.erp_dimdate&#39; table from the Gold layer given that it only contains date details like quarter, month, year etc which are already present in the central fact table. If on requires date details about a specific date, they can query those informations directly using the numerous date functions in mysql.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="273" width="830" x="165" y="824" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="_RGwDC88Wdcd8SNDBt_c-101" parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#FFA170,#FFC67F);fontColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#B20000;dashed=1;" value="" vertex="1">
